# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform, not only in the repository.
#
# Windows checkouts default to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites tracked text files to
# CRLF on the way out. For most projects that is invisible. Here it is not, because
# several tracked files are compared BYTE FOR BYTE:
#
#   obsidian-plugin/main.js   a tracked esbuild output. tests/plugin-bundle.test.mjs
#                             builds it in memory and holds the committed file to the
#                             result — and esbuild writes LF, so a CRLF checkout fails a
#                             test that is otherwise about the bundle being stale. That
#                             is exactly the false alarm a byte-for-byte guard must not
#                             produce, and it is what the Windows CI job found.
#
#   bench/corpus/*.json       hashed and re-serialised by the bench instruments.
#   models/MANIFEST.json      the record of which bytes a measurement was taken on.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` keeps LF in the working tree everywhere. Anyone editing on Windows
# gets LF files, which every editor Obsidian users are likely to have handles.
* text=auto eol=lf

#   tests/fixtures/*.tar.gz  archives written by a real GNU tar, read byte for byte by
#                            the extractor. A CRLF rewrite would not corrupt them
#                            subtly — it would make them not gzip at all.
#
# Images and archives are bytes and must never be touched.
*.tar.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gguf binary
*.onnx binary
